// Test: the edges of the integer range, which every engine holds exactly.
//
// A Zymbol integer is a safe integer: ±(2⁵³ − 1). The bound is the f64
// mantissa, so the browser engine can hold it with no rounding and the Rust and
// OCaml engines have room to spare. Before this range was written down each
// engine used its host's: i64 twice, OCaml's 63-bit int, and a double — and
// `10 ^ 20` gave four different answers, two of them wrong in silence.

// The bounds themselves round-trip.
>> 9007199254740991 ¶
>> -9007199254740991 ¶

// Arithmetic that lands exactly on the edge is fine.
>>(9007199254740990 + 1) ¶
>>(-9007199254740990 - 1) ¶

// The last power of two that fits, and the one before it.
>>(2 ^ 52) ¶
>>(2 ^ 52 - 1) ¶

// Negation is total: the range is symmetric, so every integer's negation is an
// integer. This is why `-x` is the one operation that can never overflow.
>>(-9007199254740991) ¶
x = 9007199254740991
>> -x ¶

// The precision the browser engine used to lose: 2⁵³ − 1 is exact, and the
// integer before it is a different number. `9007199254740993` printed
// `9007199254740992` there for as long as the engine existed.
>>(9007199254740991 - 1) ¶
>>(9007199254740991 == 9007199254740990) ¶

// Multiplication up to the edge.
>>(94906265 * 94906265) ¶

// A zero operand. The overflow guard has to divide by one of the operands to
// test the product before computing it, and in the OCaml engine — built with
// -unsafe -O3 — dividing by zero is a CPU trap, not an exception: this line
// dumped core until the guard was moved onto the divisor.
>>(9007199254740991 * 0) ¶
>>(0 * 9007199254740991) ¶
>>(0 * 0) ¶
>>(-9007199254740991 * 0) ¶

// Integer division and modulo never leave the range.
>>(9007199254740991 / 3) ¶
>>(9007199254740991 % 1000) ¶
